"@value": "(Flow maps in cartography are by definition of Phan (2005) \"a mix of maps and flow charts, that show the movement of objects from one location to another, such as the number of people in a migration, the amount of goods being traded, or the number of packets in a network.\" Flow maps according to Harris (1999) \"can be used to show movement of almost anything, including tangible things such as people, products, natural resources, weather, etc, as well as intangible things such as know-how, talent, credit of goodwill.\" Flow maps can indicate things like: * What it is that flows, moves, migrates, etc. * What direction the flow is moving and/or what the source and destination are. * How much is flowing, being transferred, transported, etc. * General information about what is flowing and how it is flowing.)" } ] },  { "@id": "_:b106httpc0liddOtlocdOtgovauthoritiesgenreFormsgf2011026756", "@type": [ "http://id.loc.gov/ontologies/RecordInfo#RecordInfo" ], "http://id.loc.gov/ontologies/RecordInfo#recordChangeDate": [ { "@type": "http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#dateTime",
