"@value": "p. 225 (Maps that show linear movement between places are called flow maps, and because of this quality, are sometimes referred to as dynamic maps. Symbols on quantitative flow maps are lines, usually with arrows to show direction, that vary in width ... Flow mapping began with those done by Henry Drury Harness in 1837 ... Flow maps used in textbooks are divided into three classes: radial, network, and distributive) p. 226 (can show what kind of (qualitative) or how much (quantitative) movement there is between two places; flow line maps; In the last edition of his book, Raisz places them into a class of maps called cartograms. However, not to be confused with value-by-area cartograms, flow maps are more correctly called linear cartograms) p. 239 (glossary definitions for types of flow maps: desire line map; distributive flow map; network flow map; radial flow map; traffic flow map)" } ] },  { "@id": "_:b22httpc0liddOtlocdOtgovauthoritiesgenreFormsgf2011026756", "@type": [ "http://www.loc.gov/mads/rdf/v1#GenreForm", "http://www.loc.gov/mads/rdf/v1#Variant" ], "http://www.loc.gov/mads/rdf/v1#variantLabel": [ { "@language": "en",
